1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:10,640
Welcome to Huberman Lab Essentials, where we revisit past episodes for the most potent and actionable science-based tools for mental health, physical health, and performance.

2
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:17,412
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a professor of neurobiology and ophthalmology at Stanford School of Medicine.

3
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:20,460
And now for my discussion with Dr. Matthew McDougall.

4
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:22,400
Dr. McDougal, welcome.

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:23,520
Good to be here.

6
00:00:23,886 --> 00:00:36,480
Just to kick things off, as a neurosurgeon and as a neuroscientist, Can you share with us your vision of the brain as an organ as it relates to what's possible there?

7
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:47,040
Surgeons tend to view the world a little bit differently than most because they like to fix and they like to mend, and in your case, have the potential to add things into the brain that don't exist there already.

8
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,960
So how do you think about and conceptualize the brain as an organ?

9
00:00:51,406 --> 00:00:57,840
And what do you think is really possible with the brain that most of us don't already probably think about?

10
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:06,724
The brain really is a collection of functional modules pinned together, duct taped together in this bone box attached to your head.

11
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:10,660
And sometimes you see very interesting failure modes.

12
00:01:10,926 --> 00:01:16,800
So one of the most memorable patients I ever had was very early on in my training.

13
00:01:16,974 --> 00:01:23,360
I was down at UC San Diego and saw a very young guy who had just been in a car accident.

14
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,092
We had operated on him.

15
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:33,252
And, you know, as is so often the case in neurosurgery, we had saved his life, potentially at the cost of quality of life.

16
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:42,880
When he woke from surgery with bilateral frontal lobe damage, he had essentially no impulse control left.

17
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:51,760
And so we were called back by his, you know, 80-year-old recovery room nurse saying, You've got to come see your patient right away.

18
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:52,740
Something's wrong.

19
00:01:53,422 --> 00:01:59,044
And we walk in to see him, and he points at his elderly nurse and says, She won't have sex with me.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,080
And, you know, it was apparent at that moment.

21
00:02:02,382 --> 00:02:04,560
His frontal lobes were gone.

22
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:08,868
And that person is never going to have reasonable human behavior again.

23
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:13,120
It's one of the most tragic ways to have a brain malfunction.

24
00:02:13,934 --> 00:02:33,120
But you know, anything a brain does, anything from control of hormone levels in your body to vision to sensation to, you know, the most obvious thing, which is muscle movement of any kind, from eye movement to moving your bicep, all that comes out of the brain.

25
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:36,004
All of it can go wrong, any part of it or all of it.

26
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:44,960
So, yeah, working with the brain is the substance of the brain as a surgeon, very high stakes, but once in a while you get a chance to really help.

27
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:48,292
You get a chance to fix something that seems unfixable.

28
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:53,956
And you have, Lazarus-like miracles, not too uncommonly.

29
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,200
so it's extremely satisfying as a career.

30
00:02:57,646 --> 00:03:00,420
Could you share with us one of the more satisfying experiences?

31
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:04,800
Or what qualifies as satisfying in neurosurgery?

32
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:05,360
Yeah.

33
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:15,620
One of the relatively newer techniques that we do is, if someone comes in with a reasonably small tumor, somewhere deep in the brain that's hard to get to.

34
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:23,680
the traditional approach to taking that out, would involve cutting through a lot of good normal brain and disrupting a lot of neurons.

35
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:27,236
a lot of white matter, the, you know, kind of the wires connecting neurons.

36
00:03:29,134 --> 00:03:45,200
then the modern approach involves a two millimeter drill hole in the skull down which you can pass a little fiber optic cannula and attach it to a laser and just heat the tumor up deep inside the brain under direct MRI visualization in real time.

37
00:03:45,358 --> 00:03:52,160
So you're this person is in the MRI scanner, you're taking pictures every second or so as the tumor heats up.

38
00:03:52,240 --> 00:04:01,636
You can monitor the temperature and get it exactly where you want it, where it's going to kill all those tumor cells, but not hurt hardly any of the brain surrounding it.

39
00:04:02,254 --> 00:04:04,080
And so, not uncommonly.

40
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:09,604
nowadays, we have someone come in with a tumor that previously would have been catastrophic to operate on.

41
00:04:09,934 --> 00:04:28,080
and we can eliminate that tumor with, you know, leaving a poke hole in their skin, even in cases that previously would have considered totally inoperable, say a tumor in the brain stem or a tumor in uh primary motor cortex or primary verbal areas.

42
00:04:28,302 --> 00:04:34,880
Broca's area, where we would have expected to either not operate or do catastrophic damage.

43
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:38,080
Those people sometimes now are coming out unscathed.

44
00:04:38,638 --> 00:04:44,324
In your role at Neuralink and as a neurosurgeon in other clinical settings, surely you are using machines.

45
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:53,040
It's, I think, a very exciting time in which engineers are starting to toss up big problems and go for it.

46
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:54,436
And obviously, Elon is.

47
00:04:55,438 --> 00:04:58,596
Certainly among the best, if not the best, in terms of going really big.

48
00:04:58,958 --> 00:05:07,600
To the extent that you are allowed, could you share with us what the missions at Neuralink are and what the general scope of missions are?

49
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:11,040
And then if possible, share with us some of the more specific goals.

50
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:14,720
I can imagine basic goals of trying to understand the brain and augment the brain.

51
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:19,840
I could imagine clinical goals of trying to repair things in humans that are suffering in some way.

52
00:05:20,462 --> 00:05:28,708
Neuralink and I think Tesla and SpaceX before it end up being these blank canvases that people project their hopes and fears onto.

53
00:05:29,134 --> 00:05:32,720
And so we experience a lot of upside in this.

54
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:42,320
People, assume that we have superpowers in our ability to alter the way brains work, and people have terrifying fears of the horrible things we're going to do.

55
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,740
Those extremes are not true.

56
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,228
we are making a neural implant.

57
00:05:48,880 --> 00:06:00,964
We have a robotic insertion device that helps place tiny electrodes the size uh smaller than the size of a human hair all throughout a small region of the brain.

58
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:14,212
in the first indication that we're aiming at, we are hoping to implant a series of these electrodes into the brains of people that have had a bad spinal cord injury.

59
00:06:14,478 --> 00:06:16,560
So people that are essentially quadriplegic.

60
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:20,800
They have perfect brains, but they can't move, use them to move their body.

61
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,436
They can't move their arms or legs.

62
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:29,760
And so this, you know, pristine motor cortex up in their brain is completely capable of operating a human body.

63
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:34,404
It's just not wired properly any longer to a human's arms or legs.

64
00:06:34,990 --> 00:06:45,124
And so our goal is to place this implant into a motor cortex and have that person be able to then control a computer.

65
00:06:45,614 --> 00:06:52,292
So a mouse and a keyboard, as if they had their hands on a mouse and a keyboard, even though they aren't moving their hands.

66
00:06:52,782 --> 00:06:57,700
Their motor intentions are coming directly out of the brain into the device.

67
00:06:57,966 --> 00:07:03,600
And so they're able to regain their digital freedom and connect with the world through the internet.

68
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:07,440
Why use robotics to insert these chips?

69
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:17,028
These electrodes are so tiny and the blood vessels on the surface of the brain so numerous and so densely packed that a human physically can't do this.

70
00:07:17,614 --> 00:07:35,908
A human hand is not steady enough to grab this couple micron width loop at the end of our electrode thread and place it accurately, blindly, by the way, into the cortical surface, accurately enough at the right depth to get through all the cortical layers that we want to reach.

71
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:48,868
I would love if human surgeons were you know, essential to this process, but very soon humans run out of motor skills sufficient to do this job.

72
00:07:49,326 --> 00:08:02,244
And so we are required, in this case, to lean on robots to do this incredibly precise, incredibly fast, incredibly numerous placement of electrodes into the right area of the brain.

73
00:08:02,894 --> 00:08:13,028
So, in some ways, Neuralink is pioneering the development of robotic surgeons as much as it's pioneering the exploration and augmentation and treatment of human brain conditions.

74
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:13,600
Right.

75
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:24,260
And as the device exists currently, as we're submitting it to the FDA, it is only for the placement of the electrodes, the robot's part of the surgery.

76
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:35,919
I or another neurosurgeon still needs to do the more crude part of opening the skin and skull and presenting the robot a pristine brain surface to sew electrodes into.

77
00:08:36,159 --> 00:08:41,124
Broadly speaking, you know, the mission of Neuralink is to reduce human suffering, at least in the near term.

78
00:08:41,679 --> 00:08:57,028
Um, you know, there's hope that eventually there's a use here that makes sense for a brain interface to bring AI as a tool embedded in the brain that a human can use to augment their capabilities.

79
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:00,036
I think that's pretty far down the road for us.

80
00:09:01,008 --> 00:09:03,268
but definitely on a desired roadmap.

81
00:09:03,694 --> 00:09:09,408
In the near term, we really are focused on people with terrible medical problems that have no options right now.

82
00:09:09,808 --> 00:09:17,328
So to just give us a bit fuller picture of this, you were talking about a patient who can't move their limbs because they have spinal cord damage.

83
00:09:18,208 --> 00:09:22,048
The motor cortex that controls movement is in theory fine.

84
00:09:22,768 --> 00:09:29,648
make a small hole in the skull, and through that hole, a robot is going to place electrodes, obviously motor cortex, but then where?

85
00:09:29,728 --> 00:09:29,888
How?

86
00:09:30,048 --> 00:09:35,300
Is the idea that you're going to play a concert from different locations, you're going to hit all the keys on the piano in different combinations.

87
00:09:35,568 --> 00:09:43,808
I still don't understand how the signals are going to get out of motor cortex, past the lesion and into um and out to the limbs, because the lesion hasn't been dealt with at all in this scenario.

88
00:09:44,128 --> 00:09:53,412
So just to clarify there, I should emphasize we're not in the immediate future talking about reconnecting the brain to the patient's own limbs.

89
00:09:53,806 --> 00:09:57,808
That's on the roadmap, but it's way down the roadmap a few years.

90
00:09:58,048 --> 00:10:05,968
What we're talking about in the immediate future is having the person be able to control electronic devices around them with their motor intentions alone, right?

91
00:10:06,048 --> 00:10:06,328
So

92
00:10:06,328 --> 00:10:09,928
prosthetic hand and arm or just mouse and keys on a

93
00:10:09,928 --> 00:10:12,368
mouse and keys on a keyboard for starters.

94
00:10:12,528 --> 00:10:14,660
So you wouldn't see anything in the world move.

95
00:10:15,726 --> 00:10:22,756
as they have an intention, the patient might imagine, say, flexing their fist or moving their wrist.

96
00:10:23,406 --> 00:10:36,100
And what would happen on the screen is the mouse would move down and left and click on an icon and bring up their word processor, and then a keyboard at the bottom of the screen would allow them to select letters in sequence and they could type.

97
00:10:36,302 --> 00:10:41,924
So we're using smarter algorithms to decode the motor intentions out of the brain.

98
00:10:42,350 --> 00:10:45,808
We have been able to do this in monkeys really well.

99
00:10:45,998 --> 00:10:52,804
So we have uh you know a small army of monkeys playing video games for smoothie rewards.

100
00:10:53,488 --> 00:10:54,528
and they do really well.

101
00:10:54,928 --> 00:11:02,948
we actually have the world record of bitrate of information coming out of a monkey's brain to intelligently control a cursor on a screen.

102
00:11:03,488 --> 00:11:05,088
we're doing that better than anyone else.

103
00:11:05,568 --> 00:11:14,128
Would you be willing or are you willing, if allowed, to have these electrodes implanted into your motor cortex?

104
00:11:14,368 --> 00:11:15,168
Yeah, absolutely.

105
00:11:15,568 --> 00:11:17,188
I would be excited to do that.

106
00:11:17,646 --> 00:11:23,076
I think for the first iteration of the device, it wouldn't be very useful because I can still move my limbs.

107
00:11:23,566 --> 00:11:28,356
And our first outputs from this are things that I can do just as easily with my hands, right?

108
00:11:29,088 --> 00:11:30,948
Moving a mouse, typing in a keyboard.

109
00:11:31,248 --> 00:11:39,204
We are necessarily making this device as a medical device for people with bad medical problems and no good options.

110
00:11:39,408 --> 00:11:44,048
It wouldn't really make sense for an able-bodied person to get one in the near term.

111
00:11:44,208 --> 00:11:53,476
As the technology develops, and we make devices specifically designed to perform functions that can't be done even by an able-bodied person.

112
00:11:54,414 --> 00:12:08,292
say, eventually refine the technique to get to the point where you can type faster with your mind and one of these devices than you can with text-to-speech or speech to text and your fingers.

113
00:12:08,814 --> 00:12:11,908
That's a use case that makes sense for someone like me to get it.

114
00:12:12,238 --> 00:12:19,888
It doesn't really make sense for me to, you know, get one when it allows me to, you know, use a mouse slightly worse than I can with my hand currently.

115
00:12:20,142 --> 00:12:27,728
That said, the safety of the device I would absolutely vouch for from you know the hundreds of surgeries that I've personally done with this.

116
00:12:27,968 --> 00:12:41,348
I think it's much safer than many of the industry standard FDA approved surgeries that I routinely do on patients that, you know, are no one even thinks twice about their standard of care.

117
00:12:41,968 --> 00:12:51,248
Neuralink has already reached, in my mind, a safety threshold that is far beyond a commonly accepted safety threshold.

118
00:12:51,728 --> 00:13:01,888
Along the lines of augmenting one's biological function or functions in the world, I think now's the appropriate time to talk about the small lump present in the top of your hand.

119
00:13:02,048 --> 00:13:14,436
For those listening, not watching, there's a small lump between Dr. McDougall's um forefinger and thumb or index finger and thumb placed on skin on the top of his hand.

120
00:13:14,848 --> 00:13:22,208
you've had this for some years now because we've known each other for, gosh, probably seven years now or so, and you've always had it in the time that I've known you.

121
00:13:22,368 --> 00:13:26,528
What is that lump and why did you put it in there?

122
00:13:26,928 --> 00:13:27,088
Yeah.

123
00:13:27,342 --> 00:13:30,688
So it's a small writable RFID tag.

124
00:13:30,862 --> 00:13:40,484
So it's just a very small implantable chip that wireless devices can temporarily power if you approach an antenna.

125
00:13:40,928 --> 00:13:43,968
they can power and send a small amount of data back and forth.

126
00:13:44,208 --> 00:13:48,260
Most phones have the capability of reading and writing to this chip.

127
00:13:48,528 --> 00:13:50,884
For years it let me into my house.

128
00:13:51,182 --> 00:13:53,328
It unlocked a deadbolt on my front door.

129
00:13:53,648 --> 00:13:59,168
For some years, it unlocked the doors at Neuralink and let me through, the various.

130
00:14:00,016 --> 00:14:01,296
locked doors inside the building.

131
00:14:01,776 --> 00:14:06,416
What other sorts of things could one put into these RFIDs in theory?

132
00:14:06,496 --> 00:14:10,244
And how long can they stay in there before you need to take them out and recharge them or replace them?

133
00:14:10,576 --> 00:14:11,936
Well, these are passive.

134
00:14:12,016 --> 00:14:15,172
They're coated in biocompatible glass.

135
00:14:15,630 --> 00:14:22,116
And as an extra, I'm a rock climber, and so I was worried about that glass shattering during rock climbing.

136
00:14:22,816 --> 00:14:27,376
I additionally coated them in another ring of silicone before implanting that.

137
00:14:27,502 --> 00:14:28,804
So it's pretty safe.

138
00:14:28,976 --> 00:14:29,776
They're passive.

139
00:14:29,856 --> 00:14:33,536
There's no battery, there's no active electronics in them.

140
00:14:33,696 --> 00:14:35,296
So they could last the rest of my life.

141
00:14:35,456 --> 00:14:37,668
I don't think I'd ever have to remove it for any reason.

142
00:14:38,190 --> 00:14:42,736
You know, at some point the the technology is always improving, so I might remove it and upgrade it.

143
00:14:42,896 --> 00:14:50,656
Already there's you know, 10x more storage versions available that could be a drop-in replacement for this if I ever remove it.

144
00:14:50,896 --> 00:15:09,296
It has a small niche use case, and it's an interesting proof of concept, tiptoeing towards the concept that you mentioned of, you know, you have to be willing to go through the things that you're suggesting to your patients in order to, you know, say with a straight face that you think this is a reasonable thing to do.

145
00:15:09,742 --> 00:15:12,740
I'd like to go back to brain augmentation.

146
00:15:13,710 --> 00:15:19,456
you've made very clear that one of the first goals for Neuralink is to get quadriplegics walking again.

147
00:15:19,776 --> 00:15:27,300
Well, again, just to be very clear, the first step is we aren't reconnecting the patient's own muscle system to their motor cortex.

148
00:15:27,616 --> 00:15:31,696
Allowing them excuse me, agency over the movement of things in the world.

149
00:15:32,016 --> 00:15:32,176
Yes.

150
00:15:32,976 --> 00:15:33,856
and eventually their body.

151
00:15:34,176 --> 00:15:34,896
And you're exactly right.

152
00:15:34,976 --> 00:15:36,176
Yeah, eventually their body.

153
00:15:36,656 --> 00:15:37,456
we would love to do that.

154
00:15:37,536 --> 00:15:42,724
And we've done a lot of work on developing a system for stimulating the spinal cord itself.

155
00:15:43,376 --> 00:15:50,884
and so that gets to the question that you asked a few minutes ago of how do you reconnect the motor cortex to the rest of the body?

156
00:15:51,246 --> 00:16:00,324
Well, if you can bypass the damaged area of the spinal cord and have an implant in the spinal cord itself, connected to an implant in the brain and have them talking to each other.

157
00:16:00,558 --> 00:16:16,896
you can take the perfectly intact motor signals out of the motor cortex and send them to the spinal cord, which most of the wiring should be intact in the spinal cord below the level of, say, the injury caused by a car accident or motorcycle accident or gunshot wound or whatever.

158
00:16:17,136 --> 00:16:20,736
And it should be possible to reconnect the brain to the body in that way.

159
00:16:20,878 --> 00:16:30,980
So, not out of the realm of possibility that, you know, in some small number of years that Neuralink will be able to reconnect somebody's own body to their brain.

160
00:16:31,406 --> 00:16:52,336
Along the lines of augmentation, early on, in some of the public discussions about Neuralink that I overheard between Elon and various podcast hosts, et cetera, there was this idea thrown out that, for instance, that you would have some chips in your brain, and I would have some chips in my brain, and you and I could just sit here looking at each other or not, nodding or shaking our heads and essentially hear each other's thoughts.

161
00:16:52,976 --> 00:16:57,856
Is that possible that we could sit here and just hear each other's thoughts?

162
00:16:57,936 --> 00:17:00,736
And also how would we restrict what the other person could hear?

163
00:17:00,896 --> 00:17:01,056
Yeah.

164
00:17:01,376 --> 00:17:02,496
Well, so absolutely.

165
00:17:02,656 --> 00:17:06,896
I mean, think about the fact that we could do this right now.

166
00:17:07,150 --> 00:17:15,076
If you pulled out your phone and started texting me on my phone and I looked down and started texting you, we would be communicating without looking at each other or talking.

167
00:17:16,109 --> 00:17:24,816
shifting that function from a phone to an implanted device, it requires no magic advance, no leap forward.

168
00:17:24,896 --> 00:17:35,136
It's technology we already know how to do if we say put a device in that allows you to control a keyboard and a mouse, which is our stated intention for our first human clinical trial.

169
00:17:35,296 --> 00:18:00,576
And so, you know, texting each other with our brains is maybe an uninspiring rendition of this, but it's not very difficult to imagine the implementation of the same device in a more verbally focused area of the brain that allows you to more naturally speak the thoughts that you're thinking and have me have them rendered into speech that I can hear, you know, maybe via a bone conducting implant.

170
00:18:00,896 --> 00:18:02,212
so silently hear.

171
00:18:02,606 --> 00:18:08,388
The thing that attracts a lot of us to a technology like Neuralink is that the ceiling is incredibly high.

172
00:18:08,750 --> 00:18:16,496
There's no obvious reason that you can't interface with millions of neurons as this technology is refined and developed further.

173
00:18:16,656 --> 00:18:34,628
So that's the kind of wide-band, you know, high bandwidth brain interface that you want to develop if you're talking about a semantic prosthetic, an AI assistant to your cognitive abilities, uh, you know, the more sci fi things that we think about in the coming decades.

174
00:18:35,056 --> 00:18:39,136
So it's an important caveat when you're evaluating these technologies.

175
00:18:39,296 --> 00:18:43,616
You really want it to be something that you can expand off into the sci fi.

176
00:18:44,176 --> 00:18:55,440
To what extent can these brain machine interfaces Learn and get better and better and better because it's also listening to the nervous system that it's trying to support.

177
00:18:55,760 --> 00:18:55,840
Right.

178
00:18:56,080 --> 00:18:56,880
It goes both ways.

179
00:18:57,040 --> 00:19:00,740
Basically, what you're doing is taking a very crude software intelligence.

180
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:18,148
I would say not exactly a full,-blown AI, but some well-designed software that can adapt to changes in firing of the brain, and you're coupling it with another form of intelligence, a human intelligence, and you're allowing the two to learn each other.

181
00:19:18,606 --> 00:19:24,516
So undoubtedly, the human that has a Neuralink device will get better at using it over time.

182
00:19:25,038 --> 00:19:43,280
Undoubtedly, the software that the Neuralink engineers have written will adapt to the firing patterns that the device is able to record and over time focus in on meaningful signals toward movement, right?

183
00:19:43,470 --> 00:19:52,000
So if a neuron is a high firing rate when you intend to move the mouse cursor up and to the right, it doesn't know that when it starts.

184
00:19:52,174 --> 00:19:56,868
When you first put this in, it's just a random series of signals, as far as the chip knows.

185
00:19:57,262 --> 00:20:04,260
But you start correlating it with what the person, what you know the person wants to do as expressed in a series of games.

186
00:20:04,718 --> 00:20:13,796
So you assume that the person wants to move the mouse on the screen to the target that's shown because you tell them that's the goal.

187
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:23,360
And so you start correlating the activity that you record when they're moving toward an up and right target on a screen with that firing pattern.

188
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:26,720
And similarly for up and left, down and left, down and right.

189
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:46,240
And so you develop a model semi-intelligently in the software for what the person is intending to do, and let the person run wild with it for a while, and they start to get better at using the model presented to them by the software as expressed by the mouse moving or not moving properly on the screen, right?

190
00:20:46,542 --> 00:20:55,684
Imagine a scenario where you're asking somebody to play piano, but the sound that comes out of each key randomly shifts over time.

191
00:20:56,110 --> 00:21:12,000
Very difficult problem, but a human brain is good enough with the aid of software to solve that problem and map well enough to a semi-stable state that they're going to know how to use that mouse even when they say turn the device off for the night, come back to it the next day.

192
00:21:12,238 --> 00:21:13,956
And some of the signals have shifted.

193
00:21:14,606 --> 00:21:15,520
Let's talk about pigs.

194
00:21:16,462 --> 00:21:27,280
Elon and others there at Neuralink have shared some of the progress that they've made in experimental animals that understandably evoke some strong emotions in people.

195
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:29,760
What was the rationale for using pigs?

196
00:21:30,318 --> 00:21:36,080
Well, let me say first, Neuralink is almost entirely composed of animal-loving people.

197
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:44,640
If there was any possible way to help people, the way we want to help people without using animals in our research, we would do it.

198
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:47,120
It's just not known how to do that right now.

199
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:58,560
And so we are completely restricted to making advances, to getting a device approval through the FDA by first showing that it's incredibly safe in animals.

200
00:21:59,086 --> 00:22:01,440
As is the case for any medical advancement, essentially.

201
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:16,612
You know, I think we would probably be having a very different conversation around animal research if we weren't, you know, we as a species, we as a culture weren't just casually slaughtering millions of animals to eat them every single day.

202
00:22:17,040 --> 00:22:24,132
And so, that is a background against which the relatively minuscule number of animals used in research.

203
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:34,212
it becomes almost impossible to understand why someone would point to that ridiculously small number of animals used in research.

204
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:42,960
when the vast, vast majority of animals that humans use and end their lives are done for food.

205
00:22:43,120 --> 00:22:46,596
In that context, we do animal research because we have to.

206
00:22:46,862 --> 00:22:48,080
There's no other way around it.

207
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:58,720
If tomorrow laws were changed and the FDA said, okay, you can do some of this early experimentation in willing human participants, that would be a very interesting option.

208
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:05,680
I think there would be a lot of people that would step up and say, yes, I'm willing to participate in early stage clinical research.

209
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:07,108
You already volunteered.

210
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:08,000
yeah.

211
00:23:08,480 --> 00:23:09,892
and I wouldn't be alone.

212
00:23:10,126 --> 00:23:27,716
On that note, to whatever extent possible, I think Neuralink goes Really, really far, much, much farther than anyone I've ever heard of, any organization I've ever heard of, anything I've ever seen, to give the animals agency in every aspect of the research.

213
00:23:28,144 --> 00:23:37,904
We have just an incredible team of people looking out for the animals and trying to design the experiments such that they're as purely opt-in as humanly possible.

214
00:23:38,144 --> 00:23:43,620
No animal is ever compelled to participate in experiments beyond the surgery itself.

215
00:23:44,544 --> 00:23:54,144
So if, say on a given day, our star monkey pager doesn't want to play video games for smoothie, no one forces them to ever.

216
00:23:54,304 --> 00:23:57,104
They have free and full access to food this entire time.

217
00:23:57,184 --> 00:23:59,076
So they aren't hungry, they aren't thirsty.

218
00:23:59,470 --> 00:24:04,144
The only thing that would motivate them is if they want a treat extra to their normal rations.

219
00:24:04,304 --> 00:24:09,504
There's never any deprivation, there's never any adverse negative stimuli that pushes them to do anything.

220
00:24:09,806 --> 00:24:17,764
It's one of the underappreciated innovations out of Neuralink is how far the animal care team has been able to move in the direction of humane treatment of these guys.

221
00:24:18,384 --> 00:24:19,424
Why pigs?

222
00:24:20,014 --> 00:24:25,572
Yeah, pigs are, you know, they're actually fairly commonly used in medical device research.

223
00:24:26,638 --> 00:24:31,940
more, you know, in the cardiac area, their hearts are you know somewhat similar to human hearts.

224
00:24:32,224 --> 00:24:39,588
There's a lot of different kinds of pigs that we use in different contexts when we're trying to optimize a certain characteristic.

225
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:56,004
So yeah, the pigs are, we don't necessarily need them to be smart or task performers, although occasionally we have you know trained them to walk on a treadmill when we're studying how their limbs move for some of our spinal cord research.

226
00:24:57,102 --> 00:25:02,544
but we're not you know recording interesting, say, cognitive data out of their minds.

227
00:25:02,704 --> 00:25:12,544
They're really just a biological platform with a skull that's close enough in size and shape to humans to be a valid platform to study the safety of the device.

228
00:25:12,846 --> 00:25:17,476
So again, the the pigs are really just a safety platform to say the device is safe to implant.

229
00:25:17,806 --> 00:25:21,504
It doesn't break down or cause any kind of toxic reaction.

230
00:25:21,806 --> 00:25:34,660
The monkeys are where we are really doing our heavy lifting in terms of ensuring that we're getting good signals out of the device, that what we expect to see in humans is validated on a functional level in monkeys first.

231
00:25:35,504 --> 00:25:40,464
What is your wish for brain machine interface and brain augmentation?

232
00:25:40,624 --> 00:25:44,304
So let's assume that the clinical stuff can be worked out.

233
00:25:44,384 --> 00:25:52,144
Or maybe you have a pet clinical condition that you just are just yearning to see resolved.

234
00:25:52,704 --> 00:25:53,744
That would be fine too.

235
00:25:53,998 --> 00:25:57,344
But in addition to that, you really just expand out.

236
00:25:57,504 --> 00:26:06,084
Let's say we can extend your life 200 years, or we're thinking about the kind of world that your children are going to live in and their grandchildren will live in.

237
00:26:06,766 --> 00:26:12,304
What do you think is really possible with brain augmentation and brain machine interface?

238
00:26:12,464 --> 00:26:30,052
And here, please feel no bias whatsoever to answer in a way that reveals to us your incredible empathy and consideration of clinical conditions, because that's how you spend your days is fixing patients and helping their lives be better.

239
00:26:30,318 --> 00:26:32,624
So if it lands in that category, great.

240
00:26:33,038 --> 00:26:44,612
But for sake of fun and for sake of delight and for sake of really getting us, the audience, to understand what's really possible here, please feel no shackles.

241
00:26:44,864 --> 00:26:45,024
Yeah.

242
00:26:45,264 --> 00:26:47,824
I love the idea down the road.

243
00:26:48,142 --> 00:26:59,184
And we're talking, you know, a 10-year, maybe 20-year timeframe of humans just getting control over some of the horrible ways that their brains go wrong, right?

244
00:26:59,344 --> 00:27:12,004
So I think everybody at this point has either known someone or second-order known someone, a friend of a friend who has been touched by addiction or depression, suicide, obesity.

245
00:27:12,544 --> 00:27:17,572
These functions of the brain or malfunctions of the brain are what drives me.

246
00:27:17,824 --> 00:27:21,424
These are the things that I want to tackle in my career.

247
00:27:21,584 --> 00:27:47,908
In terms of my kids' lifetime, I'm thinking, you know, full human expansion of human cognition into AI, full immersion in the internet of your cognitive abilities, having no limitation for what you think, as bottlenecked by needing to read the Wikipedia article, first to have the data to inform your thoughts.

248
00:27:49,456 --> 00:27:58,496
Having communication with anyone that you want to unrestricted by this, you know, flapping air past meat on your face.

249
00:27:59,376 --> 00:28:05,216
It's a, you know, a means of communication that's ridiculously prone to being misunderstood.

250
00:28:05,376 --> 00:28:09,136
It's also a tiny narrow bottleneck of communication.

251
00:28:09,216 --> 00:28:13,056
We're, you know, trying to send messages back and forth through a tiny straw.

252
00:28:13,216 --> 00:28:16,176
And there's no reason that needs to necessarily be true.

253
00:28:16,256 --> 00:28:20,260
It's the way things have always been, but it isn't the way things are going to be in the future.

254
00:28:20,816 --> 00:28:38,436
I think there's a million very sci-fi possibilities in terms of banding human minds together to be even more potent as a multi-unit organism, you know, as an opt-in multi-brain.

255
00:28:39,456 --> 00:28:44,896
You know, these are things that are so far down the road I can't even directly see how they would be implemented.

256
00:28:44,976 --> 00:28:51,456
But the technology we're working on is a little crack in the door that allows some of this stuff to even be thought about in a realistic way.

257
00:28:51,630 --> 00:29:04,336
To that point, I, encourage anyone who is excited about things like that, especially mechanical engineers, software engineers, robotics engineers, come to the Neuralink website and look at the jobs we've got.

258
00:29:04,416 --> 00:29:11,012
We need the brightest people on the planet working on these, the hardest problems in the world, in my opinion.

259
00:29:11,438 --> 00:29:14,628
And so if you want to work on this stuff, come help us.

260
00:29:15,246 --> 00:29:23,856
I really want to thank you first of all, for coming here today and taking time out of your important schedule of seeing patients and doing brain surgery, literally.

261
00:29:24,206 --> 00:29:24,816
Happy to do it.

262
00:29:25,056 --> 00:29:32,576
Time away from your family and time away from your mission at Neuralink briefly to share with people what you guys are doing.

263
00:29:32,736 --> 00:29:39,296
And I really just want to thank you also for being you, which is perhaps sounds like a kind of an odd thing to hear.

264
00:29:39,536 --> 00:29:47,296
But I think as made apparent by the device implanted in your hand, you don't just do this for a job.

265
00:29:47,536 --> 00:29:56,576
You live and breathe and embody, truly embody this stuff around the nervous system and trying to figure out how to fix it, how to make it better.

266
00:29:56,816 --> 00:29:59,296
And you live and breathe it, and I know your deep love for it.

267
00:29:59,376 --> 00:30:07,616
So I want to thank you for not just the brains that you put into it and the energy you put into it, but also for the heart that you put into it.

268
00:30:07,950 --> 00:30:08,736
Thanks for that, Andrew.

269
00:30:08,816 --> 00:30:09,444
I appreciate that.

270
00:30:10,496 --> 00:30:13,296
we just want to help people, we want to make things better.

